  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday) \nIssued at 1050 AM CDT Wed Jul 30 2026\n\nEnsembles continue to show the main upper level trough axis\nlingering from the Ohio Valley southward into northern Alabama\nthrough Sunday. This should keep medium shower and thunderstorm\nchances in the forecast through then. This should keep higher rain\nand thunderstorm chances in the forecast and highs more pleasant\nin the lower to mid 80s. \n\nEnsembles begin to diverge more Sunday night into early next week.\nSome guidance drops the upper low all the way south into northern\nAlabama (if this occurs, shear could be significant and a severe\nthreat may re-materialize). Others keep the upper low over the \nOhio Valley, but shift the longwave trough axis east into Georgia.\nSo the forecast into early next week is very uncertain. NBM seems\nto be taking the middle road, keeping low to medium chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms in the forecast through Wednesday of \nnext week. \n\nAt the tail end of the long term, an upper level ridge and \nsubsequent high pressure begin to work eastward into the lower \nMississippi Valley which should begin to dry out the area along \nwith increasing temperatures yet again. Highs throughout the long \nterm period are forecast to warm into the 80s with overnight lows \nin the upper 60s-lower 70s.",
